WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.176
A fixed indemnity plan pays a stated dollar amount when a covered event occurs:

00:00:05.176 --> 00:00:10.352
200 dollars per hospital day, 75 dollars per physician visit, 1,500 dollars per surgery.

00:00:10.352 --> 00:00:14.049
The payment is the same whether the bill is small

00:00:14.049 --> 00:00:17.747
or large, and it goes to the insured unless assigned.

00:00:17.747 --> 00:00:21.444
A fixed indemnity policy can qualify for an exception to

00:00:21.444 --> 00:00:25.141
some federal coverage rules if it meets the legal conditions.

00:00:25.141 --> 00:00:28.469
The important point for a buyer is simple: a

00:00:28.469 --> 00:00:31.796
fixed payment might cover only part of the bill.

00:00:31.796 --> 00:00:34.014
It does not replace comprehensive insurance.

00:00:34.014 --> 00:00:36.972
It can soften deductibles or lost wages for

00:00:36.972 --> 00:00:39.930
Elena’s coworker; it cannot replace her medical plan.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:43.986
A nonprofit health service plan is a corporation organized not for

00:00:43.986 --> 00:00:48.043
profit to provide or pay for health services to its subscribers.

00:00:48.043 --> 00:00:51.362
Historically, hospital service plans paid hospitals and medical service

00:00:51.362 --> 00:00:54.312
plans paid physicians, under contracts with those providers.

00:00:54.312 --> 00:00:59.106
In Maryland they are licensed under Insurance Article Title 14 and regulated by

00:00:59.106 --> 00:01:03.900
the Insurance Administration like insurers, with additional obligations tied to their nonprofit mission.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.956
Today a service plan may offer indemnity, PPO and HMO products.

00:01:07.956 --> 00:01:12.750
The legal form, not the product menu, makes it a health service plan.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:19.121
Priya’s service plan quote lists several products: a traditional plan that pays providers directly,

00:01:19.121 --> 00:01:25.038
a PPO with network discounts and an HMO product through an affiliated organization.

00:01:25.038 --> 00:01:29.589
Service plans also provide what the outline calls other services:

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:34.140
wellness programs, nurse lines, disease management and pharmacy benefit administration.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:37.781
Each product carries an evidence of coverage that

00:01:37.781 --> 00:01:40.966
describes benefits, exclusions and member cost sharing.

00:01:40.966 --> 00:01:46.428
Maryland mandated benefits apply to service plan contracts just as they apply

00:01:46.428 --> 00:01:51.889
to insurer policies, so the mental health parity, newborn and dependent rules

00:01:51.889 --> 00:01:56.440
you will study in Lesson 56 bind these plans too.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:01.297
The defining feature of a service plan is its contracts with providers.

00:02:01.297 --> 00:02:06.963
A participating, or qualified, provider agrees to accept the plan’s payment as payment in

00:02:06.963 --> 00:02:12.225
full for covered services, billing the subscriber only for copayments, deductibles or coinsurance.

00:02:12.225 --> 00:02:15.867
The list of contracted providers is the provider panel.

00:02:15.867 --> 00:02:19.914
When a subscriber uses a participating provider, the plan pays

00:02:19.914 --> 00:02:23.557
the provider directly and there is no balance billing.

00:02:23.557 --> 00:02:26.795
Nonparticipating providers may be paid at a lower

00:02:26.795 --> 00:02:30.033
level, and the subscriber may owe the difference.

00:02:30.033 --> 00:02:34.080
Choice of provider or pharmacy therefore has a price attached.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.687
Because subscribers depend on the panel, Maryland requires service plans and other

00:02:38.687 --> 00:02:43.294
carriers to disclose how the plan works: which providers participate, how to

00:02:43.294 --> 00:02:47.901
find a participating pharmacy, what happens out of network, how referrals and

00:02:47.901 --> 00:02:52.124
prior authorizations operate, and how to file a grievance or appeal.

00:02:52.124 --> 00:02:57.115
The evidence of coverage and the provider directory must be accurate and current.

00:02:57.115 --> 00:03:00.570
A subscriber who relies on a directory listing an

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:04.026
in-network physician is protected if the listing was wrong.

00:03:04.026 --> 00:03:07.865
Theo should walk Priya’s employees through the directory before they

00:03:07.865 --> 00:03:11.320
pick a plan, because the network is the benefit.

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.106
Line the products up.

00:03:13.106 --> 00:03:19.357
Fixed indemnity pays the insured a set amount per event and is a supplement.

00:03:19.357 --> 00:03:23.376
A nonprofit health service plan is a provider-contracting organization

00:03:23.376 --> 00:03:26.948
that may offer several product types to subscribers.

00:03:26.948 --> 00:03:30.967
Participating providers accept plan payment in full; panels define

00:03:30.967 --> 00:03:34.986
choice; disclosure tells subscribers how to use the plan.

00:03:34.986 --> 00:03:39.451
In the next two lessons you will meet HMOs, which

00:03:39.451 --> 00:03:43.916
also contract with providers but add prepaid financing and a

00:03:43.916 --> 00:03:48.382
gatekeeper, and PPOs, which contract for discounts without a gatekeeper.

00:03:48.382 --> 00:03:53.740
The vocabulary from Lesson 18 will carry you through all of them.
